<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Easter Island</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Rapa Nui</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/easter">Easter</a> Island, or Rapa Nui, is a remote Polynesian island in the south-eastern Pacific, about 3,500 km west of mainland Chile. Settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesians">Polynesians</a> around 1200 CE, it is famous for the moai, nearly a thousand giant stone statues of ancestors. European contact from 1722, slave raids and disease devastated its people in the 19th century, and Chile annexed the island in 1888.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/easter-island.jpg" alt="Easter Island" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: kallerna · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Easter_Island_5.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Easter Island is a volcanic island at the south-eastern point of the Polynesian Triangle. It consists mainly of three extinct coalesced volcanoes: Terevaka, 507 metres high, forms the bulk of the island, while Poike and Rano Kau form the eastern and southern headlands and give it a roughly triangular shape. It is one of the most isolated inhabited islands in the world. The nearest inhabited land is Pitcairn Island, with about 50 residents, and the nearest continental point is in central Chile, 3,512 kilometres away. The island was once forested, with a large extinct palm among its dominant trees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Oral tradition says the island was first settled by a two-canoe expedition under the chief Hotu Matu'a and his captain Tu'u ko Iho, who left a homeland after Hotu lost three battles to a neighbouring chief. Estimates of the date of settlement have ranged from 400 to 1300 CE, and radiocarbon work puts it late, about 1200 CE. Land clearing for cultivation and the Polynesian rat, which the settlers brought, contributed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deforestation">deforestation</a>, and large areas show palm stumps cut by people. The settlers made the moai from local stone, using red scoria from Puna Pau for the topknots called pukao and obsidian for the eyes.</p><p>The population was estimated at 2,000 to 3,000 when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> navigator <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacob-roggeveen">Jacob Roggeveen</a> arrived on Easter Sunday, 5 April 1722, giving the island its European name. His visit killed about a dozen islanders. Spanish ships came in 1770, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-cook">James Cook</a> in 1774 reported that some statues had been toppled; he learned that the statues commemorated former high chiefs.</p><p>In December 1862 Peruvian slave raiders captured around 1,500 men and women, half of the island's population, among them the paramount chief, his heir and those who could read the rongorongo script. When the survivors were repatriated, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> spread from Easter Island to the Marquesas. The population fell to a low of 111 native inhabitants in 1877. Chile annexed the island in 1888. In 1914 an uprising, inspired by the catechist María Angata Veri Veri and led by Daniel Maria Teave, sought state action against the company running the island.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Easter Island is a special territory of Chile, a status given in 2007, and part of the Valparaíso Region. The Rapa Nui were granted Chilean citizenship in 1966. The 2017 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a> registered 7,750 people, 45 per cent of whom identified as Rapa Nui. Spanish is an official language, the only such case in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a>. UNESCO named the island a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> in 1995, and much of it lies within Rapa Nui National Park, which protects nearly 1,000 moai.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Easter Island” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Rapa Nui</b>, the island that Europeans named Easter Island, is a small triangle of volcanic land some 3,700 kilometres off the coast of Chile and about 2,600 kilometres from Mangareva in eastern Polynesia, one of the most remote inhabited places on Earth.</blockquote><a href="/empire/rapa-nui">Rapa Nui · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The annexation of <b>Easter Island</b>, Rapa Nui, in 1888 extended Chile into Polynesia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-chile">Republic of Chile · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Its relatives include Samoan, Marquesan, Tahitian, Māori, <b>Rapa Nui</b> and Tongan.</blockquote><a href="/language/hawaiian">Hawaiian · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>From these islands, after a long interval, voyagers set out to settle East Polynesia from about the 11th century CE, reaching the Society Islands, the Marquesas, Hawaii, <b>Rapa Nui</b> and New Zealand.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>Dutch navigator Jacob Roggeveen became the first known European to reach <b>Rapa Nui</b>, naming it after the day of his arrival, Easter Sunday.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-5">5 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>On 16 June 1932 Dávila ousted Grove and Matte, who were exiled to <b>Easter Island</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/socialist-republic-of-chile">Socialist Republic of Chile</a></li></ul></section>
